WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.264 --> 00:00:02.931
(drums playing)

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.180
- Welcome to Fiesta Latina presentation

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:09.373
of Movimiento Cultural.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.000
My name is Kevin Diaz, executive director,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.230
and founder of Movimiento
Cultural Afro-Continental.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:19.800
We are a New Haven-based Cultural Group

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.830
dedicated to promoting
and preserving Bomba,

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:26.960
which is part of our traditional
Afro-Puerto Rican heritage.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.810
It is with great joy that we bring to you.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.510
Our virtual presentation
of Bomba music and dance

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.860
sponsored by the Yale Peabody Museum,

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.580
and it's annual Fiesta Latina.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.380
I have been involved in
promoting and preserving

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:47.380
Afro-Latina culture in New
Haven, for the last 25 years.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.180
And I am proud of how much we've grown

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.290
and elevated our cultural heritage.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.680
I hope you enjoy this history of Bomba

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.230
and I encourage you to
dance and sing with us.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.163
Bomba!

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.350
- Bomba is both a traditional dance

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:08.300
and in musical style from Puerto Rico,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.860
it's origins are rooted
in the Island's history

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.380
of African slavery.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.730
Today, it has evolved
into community expression

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.750
and celebration of
Afro-Puerto Rican culture.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.670
The music has its roots in West Africa,

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.790
a region from which slaves were captured

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.530
and brought to the Island.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.460
Many believe that the Bomba
was created in Puerto Rico

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.220
in the 1600s,

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.220
the Bomba thrived where
West African slaves

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.400
and their descendants lived and worked,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.210
which was mostly in colonial plantations,

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.410
along the islands coast.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:45.010
Whenever a dancer enters
the Bateo dance circle,

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.590
the dancer improvises while the drama

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.283
follows his or her steps.

00:01:51.075 --> 00:01:56.075
(shekere shaking)
(singing in foreign language)

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.970
(drums)
(singing in foreign language)

00:08:13.147 --> 00:08:15.950
- Today, Bomba can be
heard throughout the Island

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:18.010
and the mainland United States.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:21.110
It is recognized as a
primary form of expression

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:23.660
of Afro-Puerto Rican culture.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.270
It's also known as the music of resistance

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:30.560
in the rallies and social
justice protests of today,

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.470
you will often find
Bomba drummers, singers,

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.313
and dancers performing.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.930
Did you know that Bomba
music has been around

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:41.890
in New Haven since the early 1970s?

00:08:41.890 --> 00:08:45.240
The first fiestas, De
Louie saldia festivals,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:49.310
which featured the Bomba were
held on the New Haven Green.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:52.050
They were later relocated
to the Hill neighborhood

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:54.280
and became an annual event.

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:57.720
And I'm proud add that the
leadership and participation

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.240
of strong, fierce, and fearless
Afro-Puerto Rican women

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.523
is one of its distinctive features.

00:09:05.836 --> 00:09:08.193
(singing in foreign language)
(drums snaring)

00:09:08.193 --> 00:09:11.110
(shekere rattling)

00:14:00.007 --> 00:14:04.770
In the Bomba, there are
over 20 rhythmic variations.

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.930
The most commonly known in play today

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:11.930
are the Sica, Cuembe, Yuba,
Holande and the Seis Corrido.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:14.410
Some rhythms are more commonly heard

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.710
in some parts of the Island than others.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:20.630
Seis Corrido for example,
it's a rhythm commonly heard

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:24.703
in the town of Loisa.
(drums blaring)

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:30.900
(singing in foreign language)
(drums blaring)

00:16:59.650 --> 00:17:02.430
Let's take a look at
the Bomba instruments.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.150
First, the Bomba drums,

00:17:04.150 --> 00:17:08.270
which are called Barriles,
tambores or buleadores.

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:09.990
They keep the basic rhythm

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:14.790
while only one drum called
primo or subidor, improvisers

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.990
while following the dancer.

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:20.280
Two other instruments
also help keep the rhythm,

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:22.820
including the Cue, which are two sticks

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:26.440
that hit a wooden barrel
or the drum and one Maraca,

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:28.963
which is usually in the
hands of the singer.

00:17:30.207 --> 00:17:35.207
(singing in foreign language)
(shekere rattling)

00:17:38.219 --> 00:17:43.219
(singing in foreign language)
(drums blaring)

00:22:14.653 --> 00:22:19.653
(choir ululating)
(drums blaring)

00:22:23.843 --> 00:22:25.010
- [All] Bomba!

00:22:25.917 --> 00:22:27.630
- That was awesome.

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:28.760
- We hope you've enjoyed

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:32.090
learning about the Puerto
Rican Bomba and its history.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.030
We hope our fellow (speaking
in foreign language) out there,

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:36.610
feel proud of their African heritage,

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:38.160
and we encourage you to join us

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:40.980
as we continue to celebrate the Bomba.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:44.000
For more information
about Movimiento Cultural,

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:49.000
go to www.movimientoculturalct.org.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.703
See you at the next Pombaso, Bomba!

00:22:54.252 --> 00:22:56.919
